al national wave.", "watch": "A decisive shift in the national political environment toward Democrats, or unusually strong Democratic candidate recruitment against a weak Republican nominee, could narrow a fifteen-point deficit.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "South Dakota remains one of the most reliably Republican states in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of about fifteen points toward the GOP. The incumbent president is term-limited, so both parties nominate fresh candidates, and neither side enters with an incumbency edge. No candidate-specific fundraising, outside spending, or prediction market pricing is available for this race.\n\nWith no public polls of South Dakota and no expert ratings published, the forecast rests on the state's underlying partisan fundamentals, which point to a Republican win of roughly fifteen points. The national environment currently reads as neutral, offering neither party an obvious tailwind. That leaves the Republican nominee a clear favorite, though the absence of state-level polling keeps uncertainty somewhat elevated.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0482, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0894, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0882, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0848, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0844, "margin": -15.0}]}